{"title":"2024 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Definition_of_disphenoid)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Definition of disphenoid)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Disphenoid_in_Box)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Disphenoid in Box)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Volume_formula)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Volume formula)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Finding_the_Smallest_Scalene_Triangle_with_Integer_Sides)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Finding the Smallest Scalene Triangle with Integer Sides)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/7/e377b1e245793f98d62ce5b2322e1658f41d8af8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{disphenoid}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"17\" /> is a tetrahedron whose triangular faces are congruent to one another. What is the least total surface area of a disphenoid whose faces are scalene triangles with integer side lengths?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/d/f3d68d6f28eb88a163b9872bf5ebdef7b54919b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }3\\sqrt{15}\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }15\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }15\\sqrt{7}\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }24\\sqrt{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"479\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Definition_of_disphenoid.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Definition_of_disphenoid)\">Solution 1 (Definition of disphenoid)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Definition of disphenoid)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that any scalene <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/5/af5673c617fb40c64e371aabdddb8076e735b5b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{acute}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> triangle can be the faces of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/7/e377b1e245793f98d62ce5b2322e1658f41d8af8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{disphenoid}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"17\" />. (See proof in Solution 2.) \n</p><p>As a result, we simply have to find the smallest area a scalene acute triangle with integer side lengths can take on. This occurs with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/0/810bc0baedbccc52fc6a497d568ec41502f9dc27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4,5,6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> triangle (notice that if you decrease the value of any of the sides the resulting triangle will either be isosceles, degenerate, or non-acute). For this triangle, the semiperimeter is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/a/92abfb6ac4e4fdd3aa59c479be5affaea325d5b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{15}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />, so by Heron’s Formula:\n</p><p>\\begin{align*}\nA&amp;=\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot\\frac{3}{2}}\\\\\n&amp;=\\sqrt{\\frac{15^2\\cdot7}{16}}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{15}{4}\\sqrt{7}\n\\end{align*}\n</p><p>The surface area is simply four times the area of one of the triangles, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/c/7dce0d0aa73d1104117cc41018a1e3ec128f56a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }15\\sqrt{7}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"91\" height=\"35\" />.\n</p><p>~eevee9406\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Disphenoid_in_Box.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Disphenoid_in_Box)\">Solution 2 (Disphenoid in Box)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Disphenoid in Box)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the side lengths of one face of the disphenoid be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc6d9a6172d35127b6cb37ea92192caf7029a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />. By the definition of a disphenoid with scalene faces, opposite sides must be the same length. Then the disphenoid can be constructed in a rectangular box with dimensions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/6/48689d638ddca59030006d362cbd848607f63aa2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p, q, r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc6d9a6172d35127b6cb37ea92192caf7029a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> are the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> different diagonal lengths of the faces of the box and no two sides are parallel (someone feel free to insert a diagram). Then we have the system\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/5/b45310ac5d4fcce8cc8aefbdc70aeda5b2d99de3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p^2 + q^2 = a^2\\]\" width=\"95\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/7/fe757b3e404d63d80486b248f658fcee8201f6e6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p^2 + r^2 = b^2\\]\" width=\"94\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/a/89a020928eb737e8cca609183a4e5c58c5fa0381.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q^2 + r^2 = c^2\\]\" width=\"92\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>for positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc6d9a6172d35127b6cb37ea92192caf7029a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> and positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/6/48689d638ddca59030006d362cbd848607f63aa2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p, q, r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" />.\n</p><p>Solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/6/48689d638ddca59030006d362cbd848607f63aa2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p, q, r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" />, we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/0/1d049f238a43e72ae9915b74689a6c9abe865d2c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2}\\]\" width=\"136\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/9/219245309c11daec8b2172d1617a104d500da7ba.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q^2 = \\frac{a^2 - b^2 + c^2}{2}\\]\" width=\"134\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/5/0a59ad6446424dd355a6bd87215cd7d2e88416e7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r^2 = \\frac{-a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{2}\\]\" width=\"148\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><br />\n(Notice that, by law of cosines (or by Pythagorean Inequality), these (parallelepiped box side lengths squared) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/3/833343d60e29fbb15f6c0fde986a56a6a17f95b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^2, q^2, r^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"20\" /> each have the same sign as the cosine of the angle at a vertex of a triangular face of the disphenoid. Since they are squares and so non-negative, every angle is non-obtuse. Further, since they are squares of positive side lengths, every angle is acute. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc6d9a6172d35127b6cb37ea92192caf7029a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> are the side lengths of an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/5/af5673c617fb40c64e371aabdddb8076e735b5b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{acute}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> triangle.)\n</p><p>WLOG, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/2/7c286a6b2750b9c5c514b55b7f52660b1f66c21f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &lt; b &lt; c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />.\nFor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/3/333f869bd6cbeb14b20b71bbb69465bc89892e2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/9/9698c992334be77221be8c8c819909eca34fde9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> is less than or equal to the gap between squares greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/9/9698c992334be77221be8c8c819909eca34fde9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" />, so all such triangles are non-acute and fail. \n</p><p>The next smallest case works:  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/e/08eda1daadd91a33ab3f3bdc509da3cc52db052b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^2 + 5^2 &gt; 6^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2d0bbc4ce0850ab2a977ac7dbb936fdf40d0a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c = 4, 5, 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>Using Heron's Formula, the minimum total surface area of the disphenoid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/c/7dce0d0aa73d1104117cc41018a1e3ec128f56a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }15\\sqrt{7}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"91\" height=\"35\" />.\n</p><p>Bonus: by Heron’s Formula, the area of an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/8/ba845cfab6223ae3cc801878a09cca2addbfc5ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-1, x, x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" /> triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/0/a90ed57ea457e25392618fae9bdd6816c53703a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\sqrt{3 (x-2)(x+2)}/4 = x \\sqrt{3 (x^2-4)}/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"312\" height=\"35\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>~babyhamster\n</p><p>(Acute triangle observations and bonus formula by oinava)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Volume_formula.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Volume_formula)\">Solution 3 (Volume formula)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Volume formula)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Recall the formula for a disphenoid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/a/63ac5a93598fe7492cc9ebcb08fbe6a5da9c5372.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$72V^2=(-a^2+b^2+c^2)(a^2-b^2+c^2)(a^2+b^2-c^2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"399\" height=\"21\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/d/12d58aa29201da09d8e620f8698e3a37547f6b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the volume and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b29b358e825defa3e11b7d903d43ec31e5909a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> are the sides of each face. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/d/96d5252c386d703d0c39c9d71aa19ad0e4a05f10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$72V^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> is a positive number, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/9/c690caf5f7e6356ce8893fb1d2a10c56b9e607a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-a^2+b^2+c^2)(a^2-b^2+c^2)(a^2+b^2-c^2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"335\" height=\"21\" /> must also be positive which is enough to rule out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/c/3fc07f7d06796e2ba5ab33f5f9401445b122f318.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2,3,4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> and etc, leaving us with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/395acbacfe19a43130a493b07f085478eb7b33f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4-5-6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" /> triangle for a surface area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/c/7dce0d0aa73d1104117cc41018a1e3ec128f56a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }15\\sqrt{7}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"91\" height=\"35\" />\n</p><p>-PaixiaoLover\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Finding_the_Smallest_Scalene_Triangle_with_Integer_Sides.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Finding_the_Smallest_Scalene_Triangle_with_Integer_Sides)\">Solution 4 (Finding the Smallest Scalene Triangle with Integer Sides)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Finding the Smallest Scalene Triangle with Integer Sides)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Claim 1: The four triangles are acute</b>\n</p><p><b>Proof of Claim 1:</b>\n</p><p>Option A: Definition of disphenoid <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Option B: Suppose coordinates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/8/e48117323a342c3ee878bc01f9e1f29c1f62d455.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/0/7e091b9463bbe743aee78e5ce5752eb0177fac93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B(x,-y,-z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/3/c036fc7ee8e8b6e830d71aa4d19899460a573e89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(-x,y,-z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/d/a3d2f3deddf8da4ae6c52f52d7f6c4d7654998cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(-x,-y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/a/69ad397210fe86d147054da6630b5ae615365e7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,z &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"17\" />. It follows the figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is a disphenoid . Every face is congruent, so it suffices to show <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is acute. Note <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092a4bac4991fdb3f2ab2eb6d247e3c723c2ee07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB^2=4(y^2+z^2),AC^2=4(x^2+z^2),BC^2=4(x^2+y^2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"438\" height=\"21\" />. Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is the longest side WLOG, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/e/3eefed42d212a3392d687da84ef3ec4e25fd2e52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2+z^2\\ge x^2+z^2, y^2+z^2\\ge x^2+y^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"284\" height=\"20\" /> implying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b91309b8ae369482da652b10d3e7dd4c56a04d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z \\ge x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f13f250f1481908fb7f400238ab1c0c2b04d8c60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\ge x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />. Now, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/0/00012b6f6abb1531316ae640a9ca347c204c3fa1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB^2=4(y^2+z^2)&lt;4(x^2+y^2+x^2+z^2)=AC^2+BC^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"443\" height=\"21\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/a/58ae3a26fb65e3f21d29a675e60624a4a1974d3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB^2 &lt; AC^2 + BC^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"156\" height=\"18\" />. This is the converse of Pythagorean Inequality which directly implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is acute.\n</p><p><b>Claim 2&#160;: The smallest possible acute scalene triangle has side lengths 4,5,6.</b>\n</p><p><b>Proof of Claim 2:</b>\n</p><p>Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/a/5ba70df5a2f9630194f32f50010cd2e78318c98d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;b&lt;c$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" /> are the sides of a triangle. Then, since the triangle is acute, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/f/b3f4fe5294a136066e65f50c682f7221eaafc70a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c^2 &lt; a^2 + b^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"96\" height=\"18\" />, and the triangle inequality gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/d/85dfb547b4588026c0e75ebd15a7c3f8419e7edd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c &lt; a+b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"16\" />. We prove no triangle with perimeter less than 15 works.\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/d/48d02444a59b917b64e8bd0963b88712fc7fa0a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/5/f552f101e064c0052d57dc7eb03cea061411e301.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+b &gt; c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"73\" height=\"16\" /> forces <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/8/e18839768407bf7c18f46a5b87de9c0dfd1bfca1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c \\le b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" />, a contradiction. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/f/1cf0953f43c76a5c79f339f9aee0fc2baac51413.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/4/5e486f30ca8576f95b60e20db1cb1ac4afb651ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b \\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" /> and the only such triangle is of lengths 2,3,4. But this triangle is obtuse, a contradiction. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/1/761f953f34c7f838cd0742690928b86e9710a5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />, but the perimeter is at most 14. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/8/03866277e52110d461883b9e648ccd50906bcd60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b+c \\le 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"82\" height=\"17\" />, and because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/2/ef2b7aa7f9a5855bfd50e46edbe209e8be1b6859.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b &gt; 3, b\\ge 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" />. and the two triples are 3,4,5 and 3,4,6, one of which is right and the other is obtuse. Therefore, the minimum perimeter is 15, in which the smallest triangle has lengths 4,5,6.\n</p><p><b>2024 AMC 12A Q24:</b>\n</p><p>All that is left is to find the surface area. The semi perimeter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/f/2afee0b1ebae3c360bb8500546c918aef32dd219.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s = \\frac{15}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"57\" height=\"38\" />, so the area through Heron's is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/9/8b95200d2e949d4e8ea5df4e0a564de623a1e7be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{15\\sqrt7}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"43\" /> and times the four sides the total area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/b/75bf6f36c8f6df0393c51bc00fe3ac1523ebd290.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{15\\sqrt{7}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"56\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://youtu.be/mlsBAql6URw?si=gSIWmdZzByO1OESo\">2024 AMC 12 A #24</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://mathproblemsolvingskills.wordpress.com/\">MathProblemSolvingSkills.com</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://youtu.be/K8EdDxR6XrE\">2024 AMC 12 A #24</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A\" title=\"2024 AMC 12A\">2024 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2024 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Answer_